<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=mendelian-inheritance">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Science and discovery</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mendelian inheritance</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1865">1865</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Mendelism, Mendel's laws</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mendelian inheritance is a type of biological inheritance following the principles first derived by Gregor Johann Mendel, a Moravian monk, from experiments with some 28,000 pea plants grown between 1856 and 1863 in his monastery garden; he presented them to the Natural History Society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a> (Brünn) in 1865 and published them in 1866. Largely ignored at first, his work was rediscovered in 1900 by Hugo de Vries, Carl Correns and Erich von Tschermak. Combined with the chromosome theory of inheritance by Thomas Hunt Morgan in 1915, it became the core of classical genetics.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mendelian-inheritance.jpg" alt="Mendelian inheritance" width="300" height="304" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gregor_Mendel.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
